# Man_in_the_middle_speedometer

STM32 firmware for the CAN gateway board. Sits physically between the vehicle CAN bus and the instrument cluster, correcting the speed signal in real time while transparently forwarding everything else.

## Overview

The firmware configures two independent CAN peripherals (`CAN1`, `CAN2`), each with an accept-all filter, and bridges them bidirectionally via interrupt-driven RX callbacks:

- **`HAL_CAN_RxFifo0MsgPendingCallback` (CAN1 → CAN2, vehicle → speedometer)**
  Every frame received from the vehicle bus is forwarded to the speedometer. Frames with ID `0x3E9` (the speed message) are intercepted and rewritten before forwarding; all other IDs pass through byte-for-byte.

- **`HAL_CAN_RxFifo1MsgPendingCallback` (CAN2 → CAN1, speedometer → vehicle)**
  Any traffic the cluster sends back onto its bus (e.g. acknowledgements) is relayed upstream unmodified, so the gateway stays invisible to the rest of the vehicle network.

## Speed Correction (ID `0x3E9`)

The 2-byte raw speed value is corrected in three bands based on the high byte (`RxData1[0]`):

| Raw high byte (`RxData1[0]`) | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `<= 0x03` | Passed through unmodified (low speed, no correction) |
| `0x03 – 0x05` | `+ 0x7F` added to the low byte (half a scale step), with carry into the high byte on overflow |
| `>= 0x05` | High byte incremented by `0x01` (a full scale step, ≈ +4.2 km/h) |

This mirrors the correction logic described in the top-level project README, restoring a legally required positive offset between indicated and actual speed after a tire-size change. Note that the exact band boundaries in this firmware (`0x03`/`0x05`) are tighter than the `0x03`/`0x07` example quoted in the main README — this file reflects what the code actually does.

## Hardware Mapping

| Peripheral | Connected to |
|---|---|
| `CAN1` | Vehicle bus (rest of the car) |
| `CAN2` | Instrument cluster / speedometer only |

Both peripherals use the same bit-timing configuration (`Prescaler = 5`, `1 Mbit`-class settings) and share filter bank 14 as the boundary between CAN1's and CAN2's filter banks (`SlaveStartFilterBank = 14`), with both filters set to accept all IDs (mask `0x0000`).

## File

- `main.c` — STM32 HAL-based entry point, peripheral init (`MX_CAN1_Init`, `MX_CAN2_Init`), and the RX callbacks containing the gateway/correction logic described above. Auto-generated by STM32CubeMX; the custom logic lives in the `USER CODE` sections.
